messages:
support-bot: New support ticket INC-4321 — enterprise customer reporting that structured_output (JSON mode) responses are frequently invalid JSON and their zod validation fails. Attaching sample response we captured.

alex: taking a look. can you paste the exact model response and the original request payload & headers? need a repro.

meera: logs pasted below. model response from proxy:
```json
{
  "user": "alice",
  "items": [
    {"id": 1, "name": "widget"},
  ],
  "count": 1,
}
```
This has trailing commas and an extra comma after the array — zod.parse fails with SyntaxError: Unexpected token , in JSON at position X.

eng-matt: request payload we proxied: 
```bash
curl -s -X POST "https://api.redwood.ai/v1/generate" \
 -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
 -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
 -d '{
  "model": "rw-opt-7b",
  "structured_output": {"mode":"json", "schema": {"type":"object", "properties": {"user": {"type":"string"}, "items": {"type":"array"}}, "required": ["user", "items"]}},
  "messages": [{"role":"user", "content":"List items"}],
  "temperature": 0.0,
  "max_tokens": 256
}'
```

samir: note - zod fails at parsing step (JSON.parse) before zod sees anything. two problems: (1) model sometimes emits trailing commas/markdown/explanations, (2) customer-side zod has no preprocess. quick mitigations: set temperature=0, top_p=0; explicitly instruct model: "OUTPUT MUST BE VALID JSON ONLY. NO MARKDOWN OR COMMENTS."; use sentinel tokens around JSON and slice between them.

alex: another durable option is to use function-calling to get deterministic JSON-like output (if their schema fits). recommend trying "functions + function_call: auto".

meera: customer prefers client-side zod and doesn't want to change prompt. we recommended a server-side pre-parse that attempts JSON.parse, then a safe extraction fallback, then a light sanitizer. customer accepted trying that as a stopgap.

eng-matt: Node sanitization snippet for server-side pre-parse: 
```js
function sanitizeJson(s){
  return s.replace(/,\s*}/g,'}').replace(/,\s*]/g,']');
}
try{
  const parsed = JSON.parse(response);
} catch(e){
  const extracted = response.match(/\{[\s\S]*\}/)?.[0];
  const cleaned = sanitizeJson(extracted || response);
  const parsed = JSON.parse(cleaned);
}
```

samir: also add these to our support playbook for structured_output: recommended prompt template, required model variants (json-curated), and telemetry keys to capture on failure (trace_id, request_id, model, temp, prompt_hash).

alex: can we recommend a model variant? meera, try rw-opt-7b-json or the curated JSON profile in the catalog — they tend to be stricter. also ask ML infra to add a server-side filter that strips markdown fences.

meera: included trace info from the failing request: trace_id=tr_abc123 req_id=req_98765. attaching to the ticket.

eng-matt: owning infra escalation -> creating ENG-ML-789 to evaluate adding a strict-json wrapper around outputs and a lint step before proxying.

alex: I'll reply to customer with repro steps, the quick fix snippet, and offer to run a smoke test on rw-opt-7b-json. tagging @eng-matt to own the infra ticket.

eng-matt: on it — will sync with infra and ML teams. :eyes:

support-bot: Action items: (1) reply to customer with fix + code snippet (alex), (2) infra ticket ENG-ML-789 (eng-matt), (3) update support playbook (samir), (4) follow up once ML evaluates model variant.
